WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.590
SPEAKER: The first
key to monitoring

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:03.840
GKE cost optimization
is ensuring

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.690
that cluster administrators
have an understanding of how

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:09.960
much of their cluster resources
are actually used by workloads

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.570
in the cluster.

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:13.310
With this data,
they can take steps

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:17.503
to ensure that fewer cluster
resources are idle or wasted.

00:00:17.503 --> 00:00:19.670
Let's take a look at how
we can utilize a monitoring

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:20.750
system around this.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:24.243
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.920
As we explored in
the previous video,

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.660
the concept of bin
packing is focused

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:34.390
on ensuring that most
of our cluster resources

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:37.340
are allocated to
cluster workloads.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:39.260
Cluster administrators
want to achieve

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.500
efficient bin packing to
ensure that a cluster is not

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:45.140
overprovisioned.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:48.260
Monitoring systems will be able
to tell cluster admins if this

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:51.050
is the case by letting them
know how much of a cluster's

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:53.270
allocatable resources
are actually

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:56.850
consumed by workload requests.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:58.410
There are three
main areas we want

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:01.140
to monitor to ensure that we
are not straining resources

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.730
across our cluster.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:08.400
CPU, memory, and the amount
of pods we run on each node.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.190
CPU and memory are well-known
concepts in Kubernetes,

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:12.570
so let's start
with how we should

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:14.340
monitor how well
they're actually

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.660
allocated in a cluster.

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:19.790
Up to this point, we
have discussed monitoring

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:22.190
at the aggregate cluster level.

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:24.312
With per-cluster
monitoring, we are

00:01:24.312 --> 00:01:26.270
getting a high-level
summary of how well we are

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:28.810
doing with cluster bin packing.

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:31.360
However, the larger the
cluster and the more diverse

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.280
the machine types,
the more important

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:36.130
it becomes to monitor
at a more granular level

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:39.510
that will help cluster
administrators take action.

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:42.330
Monitoring at the more
granular per-node pool level

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:45.330
can help us take this action.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:47.910
This monitoring can help
identify target areas

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:52.000
that cluster administrators can
improve cluster bin packing.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.550
For example, if we are
isolating a set of workloads

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:56.830
to their own node pool
that is not correctly

00:01:56.830 --> 00:02:00.370
sized to fit such
workloads, underutilization

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:03.970
may not appear to be a problem
at the aggregate cluster level,

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:08.110
but it could very well be a
problem within that node pool.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.690
For both cluster level and
node pool-level monitoring,

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:14.080
we should collect these four
metrics around CPU and memory.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.600
Allocatable resources,
which references

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:20.860
total CPU or memory available
from the cluster or node pool.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:22.750
This value will always
be slightly lesser

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:24.460
than the total resources
from the cluster

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:27.370
VM due to critical
system components that

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:29.570
have to run on each node.

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:33.170
Requested resources, which
references total CPU or memory

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:37.250
requested by pods running
in the cluster or node pool.

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:40.370
Recommended resources, which
reference recommended CPU

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:42.650
or memory requests from
the platform, which

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:46.820
can come from components such
as vertical pod autoscaler.

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:51.050
And finally, used resources,
which references the actual CPU

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:55.720
or memory utilization from
pods in a cluster or node pool.

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:58.930
In this example dashboard
for monitoring CPU,

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:01.090
we can see how these four
metrics fall together

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:03.970
over a specified time window.

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:05.830
This graph tells
us, for instance,

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:08.460
that there is opportunity
for cost optimization

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:11.970
due to used cores falling well
below the requested core count

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:14.750
by workloads in the cluster.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:16.750
However, when looking at
this example dashboard

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:19.330
for monitoring memory, we
get a much better picture

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:21.700
of what is going on.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:24.730
Workloads are requesting
and using most of the memory

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:29.080
we have available, but barely
utilizing the CPU available.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:30.970
With this information,
we can consider

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:33.460
the types of machines we
are running in our cluster

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.050
or in our node pool.

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:37.580
Can we provision VMs with
the same amount of memory

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:39.600
but with less CPU?

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.170
With the appropriate
data at hand,

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:44.130
we can now begin this exercise
of answering this question

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:46.380
to practice cost optimization.

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:49.710
The third area we should monitor
to ensure efficient bin packing

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:52.580
is pods per node.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:55.460
In GKE, users can set the
number of pods allowed

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:57.890
to run on a node to
smaller than the default

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:00.560
value, which is 110 pods.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:02.300
This is commonly
used as a mechanism

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:05.730
for preserving IP addresses,
since each pod in a cluster

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:08.630
or in a node pool
gets its own IP.

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:12.600
This feature in GKE is
called flexible pod CIDR.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.330
If you set a small enough
number of pods on a node,

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:18.360
there's a chance that you
could schedule the maximum pods

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:22.200
per node without using a
majority of the allocatable CPU

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.790
and memory on that node.

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:27.150
This is also possible even
with the default value

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.540
of 110 pods per node, should
you have very large nodes

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:32.910
with smaller workload requests.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:35.490
Thus, it's important to
monitor when you brush up

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:38.070
against the maximum
pods on a given node,

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:40.530
especially if you're attempting
to achieve IP address

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:43.070
preservation.

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:44.810
Monitoring across
these key areas

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:46.610
will help cluster
admins continually

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:48.470
optimize their
cluster infrastructure

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:50.810
in a data-driven fashion.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:54.120
This is especially important
as workloads scale up and down

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:57.578
and when new tenants
onboard into the cluster.

00:04:57.578 --> 00:04:59.370
Be sure to check out
the paper we published

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:01.470
on how to set up this
monitoring system, linked

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:03.530
in the description below.

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:05.870
Setting this monitoring
up will lay the groundwork

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:08.577
for the cluster admins to
work with cluster tenants

00:05:08.577 --> 00:05:10.160
to ensure that they
are doing the work

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:12.277
to perform at right sizing.

00:05:12.277 --> 00:05:14.360
We'll cover this in the
next video, so stay tuned.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.710
[MUSIC PLAYING]

